page('research.html','Research','Learn about adaptive optics, wavefront sensing, instrument calibration and AIV at INAF Padova', '''
<section class="container inner-hero"><div class="eyebrow"><span class="small-line"></span>RESEARCH / TECHNOLOGY</div><h1>Making light<br/><span class="serif-swish" style="color:#267f86">work harder.</span></h1><p class="page-lede">Our research interests lie at the intersection of optical physics, telescope instrumentation and the methods needed to test complex systems in the laboratory and on sky.</p></section>
<section class="container research-page"><div class="research-cards"><div class="research-card reveal"><span class="r-num">01</span><span class="r-icon">◎</span><h3>Adaptive optics systems</h3><p>Atmospheric-turbulence correction, system calibration and strategies for reliable AO performance.</p></div><div class="research-card reveal"><span class="r-num">02</span><span class="r-icon">⌁</span><h3>Wavefront sensors</h3><p>Optical sensor concepts, guide stars, simulations and methods of wavefront measurement.</p></div><div class="research-card reveal"><span class="r-num">03</span><span class="r-icon">◇</span><h3>Instrumentation & AIV</h3><p>System engineering, alignment, assembly, integration, verification and commissioning.</p></div></div><div class="wide-info"><div><div class="eyebrow"><span class="small-line"></span>HOW WE WORK</div><h2>From concept<br/>to capability<span class="title-period">.</span></h2></div><div><p>Our projects sit across several stages of the instrument lifecycle: studying a new sensor, validating an optical design, aligning laboratory hardware, or preparing a full instrument for telescope commissioning.</p><ul class="method-list"><li>Simulation & optical design <span>01</span></li><li>Laboratory validation <span>02</span></li><li>Calibration & system engineering <span>03</span></li><li>Integration & on-sky verification <span>04</span></li></ul></div></div><div class="science-box"><div class="science-box-top"><div><div class="eyebrow" style="color:#a9dfdd">INTERACTIVE EXPLAINER</div><h2>What does adaptive optics change?</h2><p>Atmospheric turbulence spreads stellar images. Corrective optics help concentrate the light. Drag the slider to compare a deliberately simplified illustration of image sharpness.</p></div></div><div class="comparison"><div class="comparison-viewport" role="img" aria-label="Illustrative star field; slider compares blurry stars against sharp stars"><div class="star-field"></div><div id="ao-before" class="star-field before"></div><span class="comparison-label left">UN-CORRECTED</span><span class="comparison-label right">CORRECTED</span></div><div class="comparison-control"><label for="ao-range">Move the correction boundary</label><input type="range" id="ao-range" min="0" max="100" value="50"/><p class="compare-status" id="ao-score">Compare the correction</p><p class="hint">Conceptual visualization only — not simulated performance data.</p></div></div></div></section>
<section class="cta-band"><div class="container cta-inner"><div><div class="eyebrow">EXPLORE</div><h2>Research takes shape<br/>in real instruments.</h2></div><a href="projects.html" class="btn btn-dark">See our projects ↗</a></div></section>''')
